<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bảo Đại</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bao Dai, Nguyễn Phúc Vĩnh Thụy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bảo Đại was the last emperor of the Nguyễn dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, reigning under French protection from 1926. He was made head of the Japanese-sponsored Empire of Vietnam in 1945 and abdicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> that August. The French returned him as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> in 1949, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-dinh-diem">Ngo Dinh Diem</a> deposed him in a referendum in 1955 and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bao-dai.jpg" alt="Bảo Đại" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_B%E1%BA%A3o_%C4%90%E1%BA%A1i_on_his_throne_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bảo Đại was born Nguyễn Phúc Vĩnh Thụy on 22 October 1913, the only child of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khai-dinh">Khải Định</a>. The Nguyễn dynasty had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> since 1802, but after the French conquest of 1883 the country was divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> of Annam and Tonkin and the colony of Cochinchina, and the dynasty kept only nominal rule of Annam and Tonkin. In 1922 the boy was adopted by a former French Resident-Superior of Annam and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to study. He was the first Vietnamese monarch to receive a full Western education.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khai-dinh">Khải Định</a> died in 1925, and on 8 January 1926 Vĩnh Thụy was enthroned at the age of twelve, taking the era name Bảo Đại, meaning keeper of greatness. A regent governed for him while he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to study. He came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1932 and began to reform the court, abolishing the kowtow and the rule against looking directly at the emperor.</p><p>In March 1945 the Japanese, who had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> since 1940, ousted the French administration and kept Bảo Đại as emperor. On 11 March he signed a declaration of independence, prepared by the Japanese, that cancelled the treaty of 1884 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and proclaimed the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, in practice a client state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. After Japan surrendered, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> forced the imperial government to hand over power, and he abdicated, saying that he would prefer to be a citizen of an independent country than emperor of an enslaved one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hồ Chí Minh invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> as supreme adviser to the new Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, a post he held for nearly a year. In 1946 he left a delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>. From 1949 to 1955 he was chief of state of the French-associated State of Vietnam. He was criticised for being too closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and for spending much of his time outside Vietnam, and in 1955 Prime Minister Ngô Đình Diệm removed him in a referendum.</p><p>Bảo Đại lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 31 July 1997, aged 83, and was buried in the Passy Cemetery. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a> regarded him as a traitor, and many overseas Vietnamese opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> also judged him weak, though recent studies have questioned both views.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bảo Đại” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 9 March 1945 the Japanese overthrew French rule in a coup, interning French troops and officials, and encouraged <b>Bao Dai</b> in Vietnam, Norodom Sihanouk in Cambodia and Sisavang Vong in Laos to declare independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Khai Dinh (r. 1916–1925) cooperated closely with the French, and <b>Bao Dai</b>, emperor from 1926, attempted modest reforms after returning from education in France in 1932 but had little power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Vietnam grew out of the State of Vietnam, created on 14 June 1949 through co-operation between anti-communist Vietnamese and the French during the First Indochina War, with the former emperor <b>Bảo Đại</b> as chief of state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After a heavily rigged referendum deposed the former emperor <b>Bảo Đại</b>, Ngô Đình Diệm proclaimed the Republic of Vietnam in the south.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-26">26 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As France changed strategy to work with <b>Bao Dai</b>, the republic was renamed the Provisional Government of South Vietnam in 1947 and in 1948 merged into the Provisional Central Government of Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina">Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1950 <b>Bao Dai</b>, head of the State of Vietnam, decreed that the Central Highlands and other upland areas inhabited mainly by ethnic minorities would form the Hoang trieu Cuong tho, Crown Domain, directly under the Nguyen crown rather than the ordinary government.</blockquote><a href="/state/crown-domain-of-the-nguyen-dynasty">Crown Domain of the Nguyen Dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Huế on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7480 1552 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bảo Đại"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7930 1822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
